### Step 1: Install NGINX
First, ensure NGINX is installed on your operating system. You can find installation guides for different Linux distributions on the [NGINX official website](https://nginx.org/en/docs/install.html).

For simplicity, let's assume you’re using Ubuntu. Here’s how to set up a fresh NGINX server:

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install nginx -y
```

After installation, configure NGINX:

```bash
sudo nano /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
```

Ensure that the `user` directive is set to the user and group that will run NGINX (e.g., `www-data:www-data`).

### Step 2: Configure NGINX as a Reverse Proxy
This step involves setting up NGINX to act as a reverse proxy, which means it will take requests from the client and route them to the back-end servers (web applications) simultaneously. Also, it will forward the data from back-end servers to the client. 

#### Accessing Backend Servers
Assuming you have three backend servers (web applications) running and they can be contacted using their IP addresses (e.g., `192.168.1.101`, `192.168.1.102`, `192.168.1.103`), we will set up a configuration to distribute traffic to these servers. 

Edit the NGINX configuration file:

```bash
sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/default
```

Here is an example of a configuration that will start with listening for HTTP requests on port 80, and proxying them to the back-end servers:

```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name example.com;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://backend_servers;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    }
}

upstream backend_servers {
    server 192.168.1.101:80;
    server 192.168.1.102:80;
    server 192.168.1.103:80;
}
```

### Step 3: Distributing Traffic Evenly
This configuration will distribute the traffic evenly across all back-end servers. NGINX looks at the `weight` parameter when choosing an upstart server. For this example, all servers have `weight` set to their number: 

```nginx
upstream backend_servers {
    server 192.168.1.101:80 weight=1;
    server 192.168.1.102:80 weight=1;
    server 192.168.1.103:80 weight=1;
}
```

If all servers have equal endurance, they'll be used at the same rate. You can modify weights to adjust the load distribution as per your needs. 

### Step 4: Adding SSL Termination
If you want all traffic to the load balancer to be SSL terminated (to secure the data in transit between your clients and your load balancer), you need to set up SSL certificates. 

Edit the default NGINX configuration to add the SSL settings for each server and use SSL for all backend servers. 

```nginx
server {
    listen 443 ssl;  # Listen on 443 (HTTPS)
    server_name example.com;

    ssl_certificate /etc/nginx/ssl/example.com.crt;  # Replace with your actual certificate file path
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/nginx/ssl/example.com.key;  # Replace with your key file

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://backend_servers;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    }
}
```

